# UST.G3AR Same-Member beta_C Source and Normal-Decision Contract

## Purpose

G3AQ closes the rank of the characteristic-zero response map, but it does not
emit the affine Deligne source `beta_C`. G3AR defines the first packet that may
join those layers without splicing members, frames or normalizations.

## Required chain

A promotable G3AR source must bind one `source_orbit_id` through all of:

1. the selected smooth graph-Prym member;
2. the finite B89 detecting meridian word;
3. its integral rank-1509 representative;
4. certified Gauss-Manin transport into the common B103 frame;
5. the 248-coordinate period/residue image;
6. the selected normalized affine delta/Deligne source;
7. the B103-to-G3AL 248-row frame transform;
8. the resulting normalized `beta_C` vector;
9. the hash-current G3AQ response and a same-source characteristic-zero
   `N_full=L*N0` evaluator (or an exact certified-root operator); and
10. the evaluated 126-coordinate residual `N_full beta_C`.

The source is promoted only when all bindings are repository-local and
hash-current, the upstream source commit is locked, no observed value or fit
parameter is used, and an exact equality, zero-radius enclosure or certified
root-existence theorem proves

```text
N_full beta_C = 0.
```

A certified nonzero coordinate is a valid obstruction result, but not a
source promotion. A finite-radius interval that merely contains zero is not a
zero certificate; a separated interval may certify the nonzero branch.
A certified-root theorem must uniquely identify the bound `source_orbit_id`;
existence of some unbound root in a neighborhood is not accepted.

The finite G3AS operator supplies a rigorous modular prefilter. A nonzero
finite residual proves a characteristic-zero obstruction for an integrally
bound source. A zero finite residual does **not** fill item 9 and does not prove
the G3AR equality; the characteristic-zero evaluator or root theorem remains
mandatory.

G3AT through G3AW improve the route into this contract without changing its
acceptance rule. G3AT permits the common-frame projector certificate to use
three primal transports plus three exact polarization applications instead of
six primal/adjoint transports. G3AU permits pulled-back normal rows to reject a
source before all 248 beta coordinates are materialized. G3AV fixes each
polarization application as 1509 exact B103 top-coefficient reductions with
relative weights `-1/48,-1/36,-1/48`; the common scale cancels, but three
nonzero self-pairing certificates remain mandatory. A candidate that
survives those 126 rows must still provide every binding above; none of these
reductions is a zero certificate.

G3AW narrows the finite-word/source-extraction stage without changing any
G3AR item. Six unordered G3AV pairings determine the three-line Gram matrix.
When its diagonal and determinant are certified nonzero, the three generated
reflections fix the orthogonal complement, so their word and source
coefficients are decided on the exact `3 x 3` span. Full ambient dual rows are
required only if that nondegeneracy test fails or a later computation acts on
an arbitrary ambient vector.

## Anti-splicing rule

Every bound artifact carries the same `source_orbit_id`. A B89 vector in the
common B103 frame is not accepted directly as a G3AL vector: the frame
transform and its inverse/exactness certificate are mandatory. Likewise, a
finite theta sign, topological Dixmier-Douady vanishing or an old GF(11)
profile cannot stand in for the analytic `beta_C` value.

